FINDINGS-s2-graph-memory.md:11:| F3 | Graphiti presents itself as a real-time knowledge graph for AI agents and states that it supports incremental, non-batch graph construction: data is added to an already-populated graph without recomputing the whole graph. The repository README (the project's own primary document) is the source of the incremental claim. | reported | https://github.com/getzep/graphiti (README, no ISO date displayed on the fetched page — recorded here as a compliance gap; repository fetched 2026-10-11). README line as returned: "Build Real-Time Knowledge Graphs for AI Agents". The fetched GitHub HTML was truncated before the incremental-update section, so the incremental claim rests on the repository title/description only; see Gaps. |
FINDINGS-s2-graph-memory.md:14:| F6 | A 2026 analysis of when GraphRAG is warranted reports 19%–53% token-use reduction from a context-engineering method for GraphRAG and Agentic RAG, and reports a retrieval-generation gap in which expanded retrieval does not proportionally improve generation quality. | verified | https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.25656 (submitted 2026-06-24; accepted to ACL 2026 GEM Workshop; also published as https://aclanthology.org/2026.gem-main.40/, GEM 2026, July 2026), fetched 2026-10-11, quoted verbatim: "leading to 19%-53% reduction on token usage. Moreover, further analysis identifies a retrieval-generation gap where expanded retrieval does not proportionally improve generation quality, suggesting retrieval-oriented metrics overstate advanced retrieval benefits." |
FINDINGS-s2-graph-memory.md:37:- No ISO date could be attached to the Graphiti repository page as fetched (no dated commit, release or README timestamp was visible), which is a compliance gap for that row rather than an unknown finding.
FINDINGS-s3-project-graph.md:12:| F7 | Graphify maps code, docs and media into a persistent knowledge graph with deterministic tree-sitter AST extraction (no LLM credits), tags every edge EXTRACTED, INFERRED or AMBIGUOUS, and detects communities with Leiden on topology rather than embeddings. | reported | https://zenodo.org/records/22160887 (published 2026-05-13), quoted verbatim: "Edges are tagged EXTRACTED, INFERRED, or AMBIGUOUS so operators can separate what was read from what was resolved. Communities are detected with Leiden on graph topology—not embeddings". |
FINDINGS-s3-project-graph.md:38:| F33 | CodeCompass reports an adoption failure rather than a capability failure: 58% of trials with graph access made zero tool calls, and agents needed explicit prompt engineering to use the structural tool consistently. | reported | https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.20048 (2026-02-23), quoted verbatim: "However, we uncover a critical adoption gap: 58% of trials with graph access made zero tool calls, and agents required explicit prompt engineering to adopt the tool consistently." |
FINDINGS-s3-project-graph.md:40:| F35 | An adjacent graph study warns that graph structure can be mistaken for signal: a dependency layer inferred under full history merely restates run size, and a preregistered test could not separate the recovered layer from a degree-matched counterfeit keeping the same number of edges (scope: agent dependency/execution graphs, not a project code graph). | reported | https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.22741 (2026-06-22; v3 2026-10-06), quoted verbatim: "Second, structure can pass for signal it does not carry: a layer inferred under full history restates run size. Within a corpus, a preregistered test does not separate the recovered layer from a degree-matched counterfeit that rewires each step's dependencies but keeps their number." |
